>> title: Madras
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: chicken, indian, chiles, tamarind, curry
>> counters: Curry House, One Pot
>> aka: chicken madras, madras curry, lamb madras, beef madras, madrasi
>> pairs-with: madras-curry-powder, onion-tomato-masala, pilau-rice
>> servings: 4
>> time: 50 min
>> washing-up: the heavy pan
>> keeps: 3 days — the tamarind settles back and the curry leaves stop reading as a separate flavour

>> step: pop 1 min, until the leaves stop spitting
Pop @black mustard seeds{1%tsp} and @curry leaves{15}(fresh, on the stem) in @coconut oil{3%Tbs}(40 g) in a #heavy pan{} ~fry{1%min}, with the lid held over the pan until the spitting stops.

>> step: sear 6 min
Sear @boneless chicken thighs{1 1/2%lb}(680 g; cut in 2-in pieces) in @&(~1)tempered oil{} ~sear{6%min}.

>> step: fry in 4 min
Fry @onion-tomato masala{1 1/2%cup}(360 g; the base recipe), @madras curry powder{2%Tbs} and @cayenne{1%tsp}(or 2, and that is the rung above) into @&(~1)seared chicken{} ~fry{4%min}.

>> step: simmer 20 min, uncovered
Simmer @&(~1)curry{} with @tamarind concentrate{1%Tbs}(20 g; or a walnut of pulp soaked and strained), @water{3/4%cup}(180 mL) and @kosher salt{1 1/2%tsp} ~simmer{20%min}, uncovered, until it is thick enough to sit up on a spoon.

>> step: stir through off the heat
Stir @cilantro{3%Tbs}(chopped) and @lemon juice{2%tsp} through @&(~1)madras{} off the heat.

Lamb wants ninety minutes of braising before the tamarind goes in. The heat here is cayenne and not method.
